 # Transient Progressive Photon Beams
 
-Also called: Transient Progressive Photon Beam Mapping, T-PPB
 
 ## Overview
-Transient Progressive Photon Beams is a volumetric light transport simulation method that estimates transient radiance (time-resolved light intensity) in participating media using photon beams. It builds on [Progressive Photon Beam Mapping](7795-progressive-photon-beam-mapping.md) by optimizing the convergence rate of the estimator. Unlike standard progressive methods that may suffer from slow convergence or high memory usage, this method minimizes the Asymptotic Mean Squared Error (AMSE) by analytically deriving optimal decay rates for the kernel bandwidths and iteration scaling parameters. It achieves an optimal convergence rate of $O(n^{-2/3})$ for the AMSE, balancing bias and variance in the presence of transient noise.
+Transient Progressive Photon Beams is a volumetric light transport simulation method that uses photon beams to estimate transient radiance in participating media [S1]. It builds on [Progressive Photon Beam Mapping](7795-progressive-photon-beam-mapping.md) by optimizing the convergence rate of the estimator through analytical minimization of the Asymptotic Mean Squared Error (AMSE) [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method estimates transient radiance $\hat{L}_n$ at iteration $n$ by accumulating photon beam contributions. The core innovation lies in the parameterization of the kernel bandwidth decay and the iteration count scaling to minimize the Asymptotic Mean Squared Error (AMSE).
